---
id: org.dxos.plugin.deepseek
name: DeepSeekPlugin
version: 0.1.0
---

A **headless** Composer plugin that makes [DeepSeek](https://platform.deepseek.com)
connectable and runnable. It has no React/UI surfaces and contributes two things:

1. A **Connector** entry (`source: "deepseek.com"`) with an API-key credential form, so the user
   connects DeepSeek through the generic `plugin-connector` UI. On submit the key is stored as an
   `AccessToken` (source `deepseek.com`) plus a `Connection` in ECHO.
2. A **Skill** (`org.dxos.skill.deepseek`, `agentCanEnable: true`) that runs the DeepSeek harness
   — DeepSeek's own coding agent — inside a `plugin-sandbox` container, with that credential bound
   to the container by reference.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                     |
| ------ | ----------------------- |
| `feat` | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0` |
| `test` | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0` |
| `flow` | `org.dxos.mdl.flow@1.0` |
| `op`   | `org.dxos.mdl.op@1.1`   |

## Connector

```mdl
feat DeepSeekConnector
  desc: >
    Contributes a ConnectorEntry via Capability.contribute(Connector,
    [entry]) on the connector Start activation event (mirrors
    plugin-ideogram's non-OAuth credentialForm connector).
  id: org.dxos.plugin.deepseek.connector
  source: deepseek.com
  label: DeepSeek
  credentialForm:
    schema: DeepSeekTokenForm { token: string }   # the API key, Password format
    onSubmit: >
      Trim the token; if empty, throw. Build
      Obj.make(AccessToken, { source: "deepseek.com", token }) and
      Obj.make(Connection, { name: "DeepSeek", connectorId: <id>,
      accessToken: Ref.make(accessToken) }); return
      { kind: "complete", accessToken, connection }.
```

## Harness skill

```mdl
feat DeepSeekSkill
  desc: >
    Contributes an AppCapabilities.SkillDefinition. The skill's tools are the two operations
    below; `agentCanEnable: true`, so the agent turns the skill on mid-task rather than the user
    enabling it up front. The harness itself is not bundled — it is installed into the sandbox
    on provision, so nothing DeepSeek-specific runs in the browser.
  key: org.dxos.skill.deepseek
```

```mdl
op installHarness
  key: org.dxos.operation.deepseek.installHarness
  desc: >
    Creates a sandbox via op:createSandbox, binds the space's deepseek.com AccessToken to it as
    the DEEPSEEK_API_KEY credential (by REFERENCE — the sandbox resolves it per exec, so the key
    never enters an operation result or a transcript), then installs the harness CLI via op:exec.
  input:
    name?: string
    baseImage?: string
    harnessPackage?: string     # defaults to the plugin's pinned npm package
  output:
    sandboxId: string           # ECHO object URI of the sandbox
    installOutput: string
  errors:
    - MissingCredentialError    # no deepseek.com AccessToken in the space
    - HarnessInstallError       # the install command exited non-zero
  effects: [echo:read, echo:write, sandbox:create, sandbox:exec]
```

```mdl
op runHarness
  key: org.dxos.operation.deepseek.runHarness
  desc: >
    Runs the harness on a prompt in a provisioned sandbox via op:exec. The prompt is the final
    positional argument and is shell-quoted; model and API host travel as environment
    (DEEPSEEK_MODEL, DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL) rather than as CLI flags this plugin would have to guess.
  input:
    sandbox: Ref<Sandbox>
    prompt: string
    model?: string
    args?: string[]
    harnessBin?: string
    cwd?: string
    timeout?: number
  output:
    stdout: string
    stderr: string
    exitCode: number
    success: boolean
  effects: [sandbox:exec]
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
test T-1: Connector builds AccessToken + Connection
  given: the DeepSeek ConnectorEntry
  when: credentialForm.onSubmit runs with { token: "sk-…" }
  then:
    - returns kind "complete"
    - accessToken.source === "deepseek.com" and accessToken.token === "sk-…"
    - connection.connectorId === the entry id and references the accessToken
```

```mdl
test T-2: Empty token rejected
  given: the DeepSeek ConnectorEntry
  when: credentialForm.onSubmit runs with { token: "  " }
  then: it throws (no AccessToken / Connection created)
```

```mdl
test T-3: Install fails cleanly with no credential
  given: a space with no deepseek.com AccessToken
  when: op:installHarness runs
  then: it fails with MissingCredentialError (no sandbox is created)
```

```mdl
test T-4: The prompt is shell-quoted
  given: a prompt containing shell metacharacters
  when: the run command is built
  then: the prompt is a single quoted argument, with embedded quotes escaped
```

## QA

Executable flows against the running app. Each step carries a human instruction (`do`) and, where
the step maps to an invocable operation, an agent binding (`invoke`). Report failures by flow and
step number.

```mdl
flow QA-1: Connect DeepSeek with an API key
  desc: >
    The whole plugin, end to end — enable it, create a Connection choosing DeepSeek, paste a key,
    and see the connection listed under the space's Connections.
  actors: human
  covers: [DeepSeekConnector, T-1, T-2]
  status: passing            # 2026-08-27; recorded against the PR preview deployment
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open (the default "My Space" is fine)
    - plugin-deepseek is OFF — it is a `labs` plugin and off by default; enabling is step 1
    - no connection named "DeepSeek" exists in that space — ABORT rather than deleting one this
      flow did not create
  test:
    - name: Enable the plugin
      do: Open Plugins from the sidebar, filter for "deep", and toggle DeepSeek on.
      expect: the DeepSeek card shows the LABS tag and its toggle reads on
      note: >
        NO `invoke` — enabling is a UI action; `manager.enable()` from the debug port does not
        perform it (same finding as plugin-chess QA-1).
    - name: Open the space's Connections panel
      do: Return to the space, expand Settings in the navtree and click Connections.
      expect: the Connections panel opens, listing no DeepSeek connection yet
    - name: Create a Connection and choose DeepSeek
      do: Click + on the space, choose Connection, pick DeepSeek in the Service dropdown, Save.
      expect: >
        DeepSeek is offered in the Service dropdown alongside the other connectors, and choosing it
        opens a "Connect DeepSeek" dialog with a single required "API key" field
      note: |
        DeepSeek appears in that dropdown only because the plugin was enabled in step 1 — the
        connector entry is contributed on the connector Start event.

        The Connections panel's own "Connect" button does NOT reach this dialog — it is inert
        (plugin-connector's `handleAdd` invokes `OpenObjectForm` with a `typename` and nothing
        opens, no error). Reproduced on a fresh profile, and unrelated to this plugin; the `+`
        path in `do:` is the working gesture. Reported, not fixed here.
    - name: Reject an empty key
      do: With the API key field left empty, click Save.
      expect: >
        no connection is created — the credential form's onSubmit refuses an all-whitespace key
        (T-2). The dialog closes without a message, which is the generic dialog's behaviour for
        every credential-form connector, not this plugin's.
    - name: Paste the API key
      do: Reopen the dialog as in step 3 and type a key into the API key field.
      expect: the field masks its input (Password format), so the key is never shown in the clear
    - name: Save the connection
      do: Click Save.
      expect: >
        a DeepSeek row appears under Settings -> Connections in the navtree and opens as its own
        plank titled "DeepSeek" (T-1 — an AccessToken with source `deepseek.com` plus a Connection
        referencing it)
  after:
    - name: Delete the connection
      do: Open the DeepSeek connection and click "Delete connection".
      expect: the row leaves the navtree, its plank closes, and a "Connection deleted" toast appears
      note: >
        NO `invoke` — the connection is created by a UI gesture in step 6, which has no operation to
        `capture` from, so there is no captured connection value for `space.removeObjects` to name.
        The delete action in the connection's own panel routes through that operation anyway.
```

```mdl
flow QA-2: Run the harness in a sandbox
  desc: >
    The skill end to end — provision a sandbox against a connected DeepSeek account and run the
    harness on a trivial prompt.
  actors: agent
  status: unverified
  given:
    - QA-1 has run, so a deepseek.com credential exists in the space
    - plugin-deepseek and plugin-sandbox are both enabled
    - the sandbox service is reachable
  test:
    - name: Install the harness
      do: Ask the assistant to set up the DeepSeek harness.
      invoke: [op:installHarness] { name: "QA Harness" }
      capture: installed
      expect: a sandbox exists with the DeepSeek credential bound and the harness CLI installed
      assert: return !!$installed.sandboxId
    - name: Run a prompt
      do: Ask the assistant to have DeepSeek print the files in the working directory.
      invoke: [op:runHarness] { sandbox: { "/": $installed.sandboxId }, prompt: "list the files here" }
      expect: the harness runs and its output comes back
      assert: return $result.exitCode === 0
  after:
    - name: Delete the sandbox
      do: Right-click "QA Harness" in the navtree and choose Delete.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { refs: [{ "/": $installed.sandboxId }], target: $given.space }
```

## Out of scope (v1)

- Any in-browser DeepSeek API client / model provider; the harness runs in the sandbox.
- OAuth (DeepSeek uses a static API key).
- Multi-account configuration.
- Streaming harness output; a run returns once, when the command exits.
- Pinning the harness CLI's distribution. The npm package and binary names are constants with
  per-call overrides (`harnessPackage`, `harnessBin`) precisely because DeepSeek's CLI release
  channel is not something this plugin can guarantee.
